-\section*{Abstract}
-
-Semantic segmentation assigns a class to each location in an image,
-allowing structures such as organs to be identified and measured. In
-medical imaging, it supports the study of anatomy and disease, but
-obtaining annotations and training accurate models require time and
-resources. This thesis investigates whether spatial information already
-contained in annotations can improve three-dimensional segmentation when
-labelled data and training computation are limited.
-
-The approach uses Logic Tensor Networks (LTNs), which combine neural
-learning with knowledge expressed as logical statements, and their
-logarithmic formulation, logLTN. Four additional training objectives
-encourage correct labels near boundaries and correct label transitions
-between neighbouring locations, weight errors by distance from class
-boundaries, and balance supervision across layers of a volume. These
-objectives reuse existing annotations without requiring additional manual
-labelling.
-
-The medical experiments concern hippocampus segmentation, relevant to the
-study of memory and Alzheimer's disease, using the Medical Segmentation
-Decathlon (MSD) and Alzheimer's Disease Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI)
-datasets. Salt-body segmentation in synthetic GeoFWI3D volumes tests
-whether the benefits extend beyond medical imaging. Experiments cover four
-architectures trained from scratch under matched computational budgets
-within each dataset, and adaptation of pretrained Segment Anything Model 3
-(SAM3) and its medical specialization, MedSAM3, using small labelled
-support sets.
-
-For every architecture and dataset studied from scratch, at least one
-tested combination improves mean validation Dice, a measure of
-segmentation overlap, over the matched baseline. The largest gains are
-11.97 percentage points in the medical experiments and 6.14 points in the
-geological experiments. The best tested adaptation configurations improve
-Dice by 3.45 to 5.38 points. The preferred combination varies across
-models and tasks, and adding more constraints does not consistently
-improve performance. These findings support annotation-derived spatial
-supervision as a useful training strategy under limited resources, with
-benefits demonstrated in both medical and geological segmentation.
